This is a reproducible bug (unlike the flaky barrier). Let me probe it directly: does the short readdir self-heal on a second read (one-shot staleness) or stay stale (lock not re-acquired)? The `.posix_multi` dir from the failed run still exists.
**Decisive finding:** both nodes *persistently* see only their own 100 files (and only their own renamed file) — stable across repeated reads, minutes later. This is **not** read-staleness that self-heals; it's a **durable directory lost-update**: when both nodes concurrently inserted 100 dirents into the same directory, each committed its 100 onto a stale base, clobbering the other's — the on-disk dir never became the union. This is the pre-existing concurrent-same-directory coherency bug (my fixes didn't touch the dir-insert path), now cleanly reproduced on TCP. Let me correct the diagnosis in memory.
Now let me pinpoint the mechanism — is the dir-inode DLM lock actually handing off (BAST) between nodes during concurrent inserts, or are both caching "EX" and diverging? Let me run a minimal concurrent-dir repro and capture DLM BAST activity.
Key refinement: **10 files/node → both see 20 (converges!)**, with a `P62-RELOAD` showing the dir reloaded (shortform→block format). So the lost-update only happens at **higher volume (multi-block directories)** — the reload-on-acquire / drain-on-release covers shortform/single-block dirs but **not all data blocks of a multi-block (block/leaf-format) directory**. Let me look at the dir-modify refresh to find the gap.
